---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import SourceDemo from '../components/SourceDemo.astro';
import ExampleCards from '../components/ExampleCards.astro';
import ArrowIcon from '../components/ArrowIcon.astro';
import {sitePath, appUrl} from '../lib/site';
---

<Site>
  <section class="hero shell">
    <div class="hero-topline">
      <span class="eyebrow">A solid modeler for people who code</span><a
        href={sitePath('docs/reference/limitations/')}
        class="status-pill"><i></i> Prototype 01</a
      >
    </div>
    <h1>Code and geometry,<br /><span>connected.</span></h1>
    <div class="hero-bottom">
      <p>
        Build precise 3D models with TypeScript. Reach into the geometry to
        inspect, select, and adjust. Every edit comes back as readable code.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a
          class="button"
          href={appUrl('website/first-model.ts')}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">Start building <ArrowIcon /></a
        ><a
          class="text-link"
          href={sitePath('docs/getting-started/first-model/')}
          >Make your first model <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
    <SourceDemo />
    <div class="hero-footnote">
      <span>ONE MODEL. ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH.</span><p>
        Click a source context above. See exactly the object it produces.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="connection shell">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Code and geometry, connected</p><h2>
        Keep your train<br />of thought.
      </h2><p>
        Some ideas are easier to write.<br />Some are easier to point at. Code3D
        brings them together.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="workflow">
      <article>
        <span class="step-number">01 / WRITE</span><h3>
          Think in TypeScript.
        </h3><p>
          Use variables, functions, modules, and browser-compatible npm packages
          to make precise, reusable models.
        </p><code>const base = box(36, 4, 24);</code>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="step-number">02 / INSPECT</span><h3>
          Follow any expression.
        </h3><p>
          Move through the source to see an individual part, an intermediate
          result, or the assembled model. Step through a relation to see each
          contact, offset, and rotation as it happens.
        </p><div class="inline-context" aria-hidden="true">
          group([<mark>base</mark>, post]) <span>→ geometry</span>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span class="step-number">03 / ADJUST</span><h3>
          Let the geometry help.
        </h3><p>
          Pick edges and faces, position parts, and adjust parameters in the
          viewport. Your changes return to the same TypeScript source.
        </p><a class="text-link" href={sitePath('docs/guides/topology/')}
          >See how selection works →</a
        >
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="examples-section shell">
    <div class="section-top">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Built with Code3D</p><h2>
          Small models.<br />Real possibilities.
        </h2>
      </div><a class="text-link" href={sitePath('examples/')}
        >Explore the examples →</a
      >
    </div>
    <ExampleCards />
  </section>
  <section class="foundations shell">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">A familiar foundation</p><h2>
        Precise geometry.<br />Ordinary source files.
      </h2>
    </div>
    <div class="foundation-grid">
      <article>
        <h3>OpenCascade solids</h3><p>
          Build with B-Rep geometry, lofts, boolean operations, fillets, and
          chamfers. Hollow solids with uniform walls and choose their openings.
          <a href={sitePath('docs/guides/shells/')}>Make a hollow part →</a>
        </p>
      </article><article>
        <h3>Typed model interfaces</h3><p>
          Expose named bounds and topology for the next model to use. Position
          parts against directional bounds, align points, curves, and surfaces,
          and rotate around a chosen pivot or axis.
          <a href={sitePath('docs/guides/relations/')}
            >Position, align, and rotate parts →</a
          >
        </p>
      </article><article>
        <h3>Your project, in source</h3><p>
          Start in the browser, then open your own project folder. Keep working
          with your files, editor, and installed npm packages.
          <a href={sitePath('docs/getting-started/files/')}
            >Work with a project →</a
          >
        </p>
      </article><article>
        <h3>Take the model with you</h3><p>
          Export the geometry you are inspecting as STEP, STL, or 3MF, or
          capture the viewport as a PNG.
          <a href={sitePath('docs/guides/exporting/')}>Export your model →</a>
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="closing shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Start with something simple</p><h2>
      A few lines.<br />Something you can reach into.
    </h2>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a
        class="button"
        href={appUrl('website/first-model.ts')}
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener">Open Code3D <ArrowIcon /></a
      ><a class="text-link" href={sitePath('docs/')}>Read the docs →</a>
    </div>
    <p class="closing-note">
      Prototype 01 · APIs and project behavior are evolving.<br /><a
        href={sitePath('docs/reference/limitations/')}
        >Explore the current capabilities and limitations.</a
      >
      <br />Read, build, and modify Code3D under its <a
        href={sitePath('license.txt')}>community license</a
      >. Commercial competition requires permission.
    </p>
  </section>
</Site>
